import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Every project/ artifact declares its metadata in YAML frontmatter","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Every `project/` artifact declares its metadata in YAML frontmatter","type":"adr","date":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z","updated":"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z","status":"accepted","refs":["ADR-0161","ADR-0162"],"applies_to":"all repos with a `project` branch; `host` (`project/adrs/TEMPLATE.md`, `scripts/project-frontmatter.py`, `scripts/gen-adr-index.py`)"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0163-project-artifact-frontmatter.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0163-project-artifact-frontmatter.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0163-project-artifact-frontmatter.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0163-every-project-artifact-declares-its-metadata-in-yaml-frontmatter" tabindex="-1">ADR-0163: Every <code>project/</code> artifact declares its metadata in YAML frontmatter <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0163-every-project-artifact-declares-its-metadata-in-yaml-frontmatter" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0163: Every project/ artifact declares its metadata in YAML frontmatter”">​</a></h1><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><ol><li>Every Markdown artifact under <code>project/</code> opens with a YAML header. Three keys are required everywhere: <code>title</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>date</code>.</li><li><code>type</code> names the ADR-0162 taxonomy entry that owns the file — <code>adr</code>, <code>design</code>, <code>rfc</code>, <code>task</code>, <code>note</code>, <code>report</code>, <code>retro</code>, <code>plan</code>, <code>backlog</code>, <code>reference</code>, <code>principle</code>, <code>goal</code>, <code>readme</code>. <code>index</code> (generated) and <code>template</code> (skeleton) are the only exceptions; they need no <code>date</code>.</li><li><code>date</code> is ISO-8601 (<code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>, or <code>YYYY-MM</code> when only the month is known): the day the artifact was created — the decision date for an ADR, the measurement date for a report or retro, the start of an investigation for a note. <strong>The date never moves into the filename.</strong> Filenames are stable labels: renaming breaks links, vendoring allowlists and citations.</li><li>ADRs carry <code>status</code> from the closed vocabulary <code>proposed | accepted | implemented | landed | superseded | absorbed | rejected | unrecorded</code>. A legacy record with no stated status is <code>unrecorded</code> — never guessed.</li><li>A supersession is structured, never prose: <code>status: superseded</code> plus <code>superseded_by: [ADR-NNNN]</code>; absorption uses <code>absorbed_into</code>. The record being replaced lists <code>supersedes: [ADR-NNNN]</code>. A <code>Superseded by</code> / <code>Supersedes</code> / <code>Absorbed into</code> sentence is promoted to the field and deleted; only rationale that does not name the relation stays in <code>status_note</code>.</li><li><strong>Status lives only in the header.</strong> ADRs and RFCs use <code>status</code> (the enum in rule 4) plus an optional <code>status_note</code>; every other type (<code>plan</code>, <code>task</code>, <code>design</code>, <code>note</code>, <code>report</code>, <code>retro</code>) uses <code>status_note</code> verbatim, because it has no enum. A task&#39;s per-step statuses inside its own sections are progress, not the document&#39;s status.</li><li><code>refs</code> is a list of ADR ids the document elaborates or relies on. <code>applies_to</code> is one prose line naming where a decision binds, when that is wider than the repo. <code>status_note</code> is the only other free-text field.</li><li>The body never carries status. Template bullets (<code>- Status:</code>, <code>- Date:</code>, <code>- Repos:</code>, <code>- Supersedes:</code>) are migrated into frontmatter and removed; a legacy <code>## Status</code> section (every occurrence) and a header <code>Status:</code> / <code>**Status**:</code> line are moved wholesale into <code>status_note</code> and deleted — except a supersession sentence, which goes to the relation fields. Compound metadata lines (<code>**Date**: … . **ADR**: …</code>) keep their non-date fields — migrate, do not rewrite.</li><li><code>title</code> is the H1 without its identifier prefix (<code>ADR-NNNN: </code>, <code>RFC-NNNN: </code>, <code>Task: </code>); the H1 itself is unchanged, so existing anchors and links keep working.</li><li><code>scripts/project-frontmatter.py</code> enforces the schema (<code>--write</code> migrates), <code>scripts/check-adrs.py</code> reads status and supersession from the header, and <code>scripts/gen-adr-index.py</code> renders the registry&#39;s date column from it.</li></ol><h2 id="why" tabindex="-1">Why <a class="header-anchor" href="#why" aria-label="Permalink to “Why”">​</a></h2><p>The date rule sent dates into filenames (<code>notes/2026-08-24-tree-shaking.md</code>), which makes every artifact rename on import — and the renames already broke <code>vendor-docs.py</code>&#39;s exclusion for a graphopt note that must not be published. The metadata itself has four shapes: 28 ADRs use a <code>- Status:</code> / <code>**Status**:</code> field, <strong>159 carry status only as a <code>## Status</code> section</strong>, and 2 say nothing resolvable at all; 365 of 469 artifacts have no date outside git history. All of it is prose, none of it queryable — and a status can disagree with the body two sections below. One header makes the corpus queryable, the checks mechanical, and the generated indexes honest.</p><h2 id="not-doing" tabindex="-1">Not doing <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-doing" aria-label="Permalink to “Not doing”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Filename dates as the source of truth</strong> — rename on import, no month precision, and the rename itself silently broke vendoring.</li><li><strong>Parsing <code>## Status</code> prose as the status</strong> — 159 styles, no enumeration; the section stays as prose, the header carries the decision.</li><li><strong>A per-repo metadata registry</strong> — drifts by construction (the hand-kept <code>docs-split.yaml</code> already lost ADR-0158 and ADR-0159).</li><li><strong>YAML for everything</strong> — eleven keys, closed; anything else is body prose.</li><li><strong>Keeping a <code>## Status</code> section beside the header</strong> — two homes, and the header is what tooling reads; the section is moved, not mirrored.</li><li><strong>Keeping the <code>Superseded by ADR-N</code> sentence in <code>status_note</code></strong> — the relation is a link, not a sentence; prose duplicates drift from the field.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li>467 hand-written artifacts gain a header. Dates come from an explicit <code>Date:</code> line in 86, a filename prefix in 17, git&#39;s first-add commit in 357, and the file mtime in 7 (uncommitted files) — no date is invented, and the migration reports which source each one used. Two ADRs stay <code>unrecorded</code> until someone reads them.</li><li>The 13 renames made for the filename rule are reverted; the graphopt vendoring exclusion (<code>notes/repo-split-plan.md</code>) works again.</li><li>200 <code>## Status</code> sections (including qualified headings such as <code>## Status of this ADR</code>) and 36 header status fields leave the bodies: their prose becomes <code>status_note</code> (217 files). No body carries status any more, so the header is the only place to look — or to change it.</li><li>20 status notes named a supersession. 16 were promoted to <code>superseded_by</code> / <code>supersedes</code> (or <code>absorbed_into</code>) and the sentence deleted; a note that was pure relation is gone, and only rationale survives. The remaining four are a partial supersession, a rewrite note, or a reference — no single field can represent them, so their prose stays.</li><li><code>adrs/TEMPLATE.md</code> grows a header, and <code>write-adr</code> points at it, so new records start compliant.</li><li>Both generated indexes gain status + date columns, which is the first question anyone asks of the corpus.</li></ul><h2 id="enforcement" tabindex="-1">Enforcement <a class="header-anchor" href="#enforcement" aria-label="Permalink to “Enforcement”">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>host/scripts/project-frontmatter.py</code> — required keys, type-per-path, date format, status vocabulary, supersession links, title/H1 drift.</li><li><code>host/scripts/check-adrs.py</code> — reads <code>status</code> / <code>superseded_by</code> from the header.</li><li><code>host/scripts/gen-adr-index.py</code>, <code>docs/scripts/gen-docs-index.py</code> — the rendered registry.</li></ul><h2 id="revisit-if" tabindex="-1">Revisit if <a class="header-anchor" href="#revisit-if" aria-label="Permalink to “Revisit if”">​</a></h2><ul><li>A second consumer needs a field (owner, milestone) — add it here rather than in one repo&#39;s header.</li><li>More than 5% of ADRs are still <code>unrecorded</code> after a triage pass: the status rule is not being applied and the vocabulary needs to change.</li></ul>`,13)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};